      "writtenOn": "2024-12-09T05:03:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is another way to write the interface which might be even less ambiguous and easier to use:\n\n```\n// @param flags array of power-of-two integer flags to set or unset. A flag to set\n// should be passed as is as a power-of-two value, and a flag to remove should be\n// passed inversed as -1 with a single bit down.\nsetInterfaceFlags(@NonNull String interfaceName, int[] flags)\n//or\n\nsetInterfaceFlags(@NonNull String interfaceName, int... flags)\n```\n\nThen we can do:\n```\nint flags \u003d 0\nint changes \u003d 0;\nfor (f in flags) {\n  if (Integer.bitCount(f) \u003d\u003d 1) {\n    flags |\u003d f;\n    change |\u003d f;\n  } else if (Integer.bitCount(~f) \u003d\u003d 1) {\n    change |\u003d ~f;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis avoid any redundancy like for instance:\n```\nsetInterfaceFlag(\"eth0\", IFF_UP, ~IFF_BROADCAST);\n```",
      "parentUuid": "9392bda0_d6861d44",
      "range": {
